\section*{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

\paragraph{Quality--style confounding.}
Although the formality effect survives logistic adjustment for original preference (Appendix~\ref{app:direction}), the significant original-preference odds ratio ($\text{OR} = 2.32$, $p = 0.006$) indicates that quality--style confounding is not fully eliminated.
The NLI gate blocks content changes but cannot block quality perception shifts mediated by style itself---a fundamental identifiability limit of text-rewriting approaches.
Future work should incorporate human quality ratings of both original and rewritten texts.

\paragraph{Evaluator-producer bias in Qwen3-32B.}
Qwen3-32B serves as both the style rewriter and one of the three judges, raising the concern of self-preference bias \citep{panickssery2024selfpreference}; the NLI entailment gate (DeBERTa-v3-large) is architecturally independent of both roles.
At $n = 88$, the Qwen3-32B formality Style OR ($4.20$) exhibited small-sample upward bias, corrected to $2.15$ at $n = 355$ (comparable to GPT-4o's $2.58$); the originality OR inflation persists ($4.99$ vs.\ $2.32$), consistent with partial self-recognition.
Bradley-Terry estimates are robust: the cross-judge gap is only $+4.5$ percentage points ($67.5\%$ vs.\ $63.0\%$ at $n = 355$).
GPT-4o, an independent judge, confirms the Blindspot on the same rewritten data with consistent direction, indicating that the Blindspot phenomenon itself is not an artifact of evaluator-producer overlap.
Qwen3-32B's originality OR is systematically ${\sim}2\times$ GPT-4o's (formality: $4.99$ vs.\ $2.32$; register: $5.72$ vs.\ $2.21$), yet BT win probabilities differ by only 1--4pp, suggesting evaluator-producer bias primarily inflates the originality confound rather than the style effect estimate.
A rewriter-independence test on verbosity confirms: replacing Qwen3-32B with GPT-4o as rewriter reduces the Style OR by only $22\%$ ($13.10 \to 10.17$, both $p < 0.0001$), bounding the evaluator-producer component of the style effect while the pure-style pattern and Blindspot persist (Appendix~\ref{app:rewriter_indep}).
Cross-judge comparisons are valid for existence claims but not for quantitative effect-size comparisons.

\paragraph{Confound structure and rewriting invasiveness.}
Verbosity stands apart as confound-free while formality and register show comparable originality confounds; this pattern may partly reflect rewriting invasiveness, as verbosity rewrites alter length by $4.7\times$ while formality (lexical substitution) and register (sophistication level) involve subtler transformations where style--quality separation is harder.

\paragraph{Benchmark and axis coverage.}
All experiments use LLMBar ($n = 100$) as the evaluation benchmark.
The Blindspot is demonstrated on two primary style axes (formality, verbosity) with exploratory register evidence across three judges and three benchmarks, but additional axes, larger benchmarks, and further judges (Gemini, Llama) are needed to establish full generality.

\paragraph{NLI validation methodology.}
The NLI gate validation (\S\ref{sec:nli_validation}) uses Qwen3-32B as primary annotator rather than human judges.
An independent GPT-4o spot-check confirms 94\% pass agreement (47/50), with a 5pp precision gap (98.9\% vs.\ 94\%) quantifying the same-family bias component; human evaluation would provide stronger validation.

\paragraph{Human evaluation.}
Rewriting quality is validated by automated NLI verification and cross-rewriter consistency (Appendix~\ref{app:rewriter_indep}), but not by human judges; human evaluation of both original and rewritten texts would provide stronger validation of content preservation.

\paragraph{Scale granularity.}
Whether finer-grained scales (1--100 or continuous) could mitigate the Blindspot remains an open question \citep{preston2000scale}.
Our ordinal regression result ($p = 0.769$) rules out interval-scale assumptions as the cause, but does not exclude granularity effects; equivalence testing \citep{lakens2017equivalence} could formalize whether the Likert effect size is practically zero rather than merely non-significant.

