\section*{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

\paragraph{Quality--style confounding and construct validity.}
The NLI gate blocks content changes but cannot block quality perception shifts mediated by style itself---a fundamental identifiability limit of text-rewriting approaches.
Although the formality effect survives logistic adjustment for original preference ($\text{OR} = 2.32$, $p = 0.006$; Appendix~\ref{app:direction}), residual confounding is not fully eliminated.
An alternative interpretation of the Blindspot is that Likert and pairwise modes measure different constructs: Likert captures absolute quality difference while pairwise captures ordinal preference. Under this view, the divergence reflects construct difference rather than measurement failure. We consider this unlikely because seven statistical methods applied to identical data all agree on the direction of the effect (Table~\ref{tab:sensitivity}), but the possibility cannot be excluded without human ground-truth ratings.

\paragraph{Evaluator-producer bias and generalizability.}
Qwen3-32B serves as both rewriter and judge, raising self-preference concerns \citep{panickssery2024selfpreference}.
BT estimates are robust (cross-judge gap $+4.5$pp at $n = 355$), and GPT-4o independently confirms the Blindspot; however, cross-judge effect-size comparisons are not valid.
Register results carry the highest evaluator-producer risk and should be treated as exploratory.
All experiments use LLMBar ($n = 100$); additional axes, larger benchmarks, and further judges (Gemini, Llama) are needed.
Whether findings transfer to settings with longer outputs, domain-specific evaluation, or human judges requires further investigation.

\paragraph{Validation methodology and scale design.}
NLI validation uses Qwen3-32B as annotator (GPT-4o spot-check confirms 94\% agreement); human evaluation of both original and rewritten texts would strengthen content-preservation claims.
Whether finer-grained scales \citep{preston2000scale} could mitigate the Blindspot remains open; equivalence testing \citep{lakens2017equivalence} could formalize whether the Likert effect size is practically zero.

\section{Claude Sonnet 4 Equivalence Testing}
\label{app:claude_tost}

Claude shows no significant bias on either axis (BT $p = 0.091$, $p = 0.284$).
Formal equivalence testing \citep[TOST;][]{lakens2017equivalence} at $\Delta = 10$pp is underpowered at $n = 87$; at $\Delta = 15$pp both axes confirm equivalence, bounding any Claude style preference to ${<}15$pp---substantively distinct from GPT-4o ($13$--$23$pp) and Qwen3-32B ($17$--$27$pp).

